// t-gv3n: mid-suite bs2 rebuilds are forbidden — the bs2 executing the
// suite IS the compiler under test. Two invariants guard that:
//
//   1. The orchestrator stamps AVRA_SKIP_ENSURE_BS2=1 into the suite's
//      process env (run_test_command), so any test's shell-out to
//      scripts/diagnose.sh short-circuits ensure_bs2 outright.
//   2. Even without the stamp, ensure_bs2's staleness check must be
//      HONEST: source_newer_than once hardcoded BSD `stat -f %m`, which
//      fails on Linux and fell back to "stale" — so every diagnose.sh
//      invocation rebuilt + relinked build/bs2, concurrently, while
//      live shards exec'd it (the 4009/4010 comptime_jit_string flake).
//
// The second spec asserts the honest check via the read-only
// --bs2-stale-check probe (env -u strips the stamp so the real check
// runs). If this spec fails with `stale`, your build/bs2 genuinely
// predates a compiler-source edit — rebuild it; every other test in
// the run is testing a stale compiler.

extern fn avra_shell_exec(cmd: string) -> string
extern fn avra_process_env_get(key: string) -> string

spec "the suite forbids mid-run bs2 rebuilds (t-gv3n)" {
    then "the orchestrator stamped AVRA_SKIP_ENSURE_BS2 into the test env" {
        avra_process_env_get("AVRA_SKIP_ENSURE_BS2") == "1"
    }
    // `tie` is accepted alongside `fresh`, and the distinction is the point:
    // `stale` must mean a source is GENUINELY newer than build/bs2, which is
    // the mid-run-rebuild hazard this file guards. A same-second tie is not
    // that — ordering is merely unknowable, ensure_bs2 rebuilds anyway, and
    // nothing predates anything.
    //
    // Before the split this arm asserted `fresh` alone and went red on a tie,
    // with nothing in the diff to explain it: a container restart had
    // normalised mtimes, and an incremental build finishing inside the same
    // second as its source write does the same on any quick machine (t-kdyj.7).
    then "the un-stamped staleness probe does not report a genuinely-fresh bs2 as stale" {
        let out = avra_shell_exec("env -u AVRA_SKIP_ENSURE_BS2 bash scripts/diagnose.sh --bs2-stale-check 2>&1 || true")
        out.contains("fresh") || out.contains("tie")
    }
}

// The `*_test.av` exclusion in source_newer_than is pinned in
// packages/std-avrac/src/build/tests/source_newer_than_test.av, against a
// SANDBOX source tree.
//
// It was first written here, as `touch tests/suite_bs2_guard_test.av` followed
// by the --bs2-stale-check probe. That arm COULD NOT FAIL: source_newer_than
// scans CLI_SRC_DIR and LIB_SRC_DIR only, and `tests/` is under neither, so
// touching this file is invisible to it with or without the exclusion. It
// nonetheless went red when the exclusion was reverted — because a DIFFERENT
// file edited in the same change (a `*_test.av` under packages/std-avrac/src)
// was newer than bs2. So the mutation check established that the spec could go
// red, not that it went red for its own reason, which is the only thing that
// makes a guard a guard. Raised in review of #1136.
